# =============================================================
# Shared Memory Framework — FRAMEWORK Configuration
#
# This is the FRAMEWORK env. It lives in the framework folder and is read by
# the gateway/daemons AND by docker-compose (data paths + DB passwords below):
#     shared-memory/.env         ← live values (gitignored, NEVER commit)
#     shared-memory/.env.example ← this sanitized template (committed)
# The CLIENT token lives separately in the skill folder's .env (AGENT_TOKEN);
# see shared-memory-skill/shared-memory/.env.example.
#
# First-time setup: run  bash shared-memory/scripts/install_framework.sh
# (prompts for the values below, writes shared-memory/.env, creates data dirs).
# Or copy this file to shared-memory/.env and fill in the values by hand.
# =============================================================

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Infrastructure — host paths for docker-compose (postgres_neo4j_limits.yaml).
# These are substituted into the compose at `docker compose --env-file
# shared-memory/.env ...`. No trailing slash. README §5 (Docker Compose).
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Neo4j host dir — compose mounts {data,logs,import,plugins} underneath it.
NEO4J_HOST_DIR=/your/data/path/neo4j
# Postgres data dir.
PG_DATA_DIR=/your/data/path/postgres
# GGUF model dir for the bundled BGE-M3 embedder + reranker containers.
# Only needed if you run the compose inference layer (skip if you use LM Studio).
LLM_MODELS_DIR=/path/to/your/LLM_Models

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — you must set these. README §5 (Docker Compose) / §6 (Schema).
# -------------------------------------------------------------
NEO4J_PASSWORD=your-neo4j-password-here
PG_PASSWORD=your-postgres-password-here

# Optional — only if you use the LM Studio web-search MCP. README §16.
# Consumed by mcp.json → tavily-mcp; the Python stack does not read it.
TAVILY_API_KEY=your-tavily-api-key-here

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication — README §10 (Token setup) and SECURITY.md
# -------------------------------------------------------------

# GATEWAY .env: the agent token registry. Empty = auth DISABLED (all
# requests accepted — the backward-compatible default). Generate with:
#   uv run python shared-memory/scripts/generate_tokens.py
# Format: comma-separated name:token pairs, one per agent.
AGENT_TOKENS=

# GATEWAY .env: optional read-only roles. Confines the named tokens to
# GET /health, GET /memory/telemetry, and POST /memory/graph (read-only
# Cypher) — every other route returns 403. Use for ops clients that must
# observe but never write, e.g. the shared-memory-monitor dashboard.
# Empty / unset = every token keeps full read/write (backward compatible).
# Format: comma-separated name:role pairs; role is "read" or "full".
# AGENT_ROLES=monitor:read

# AGENT skill .env (NOT the gateway): this agent's own token. Each agent
# uses a distinct token — never shared. LM Studio: set it in the mcp.json
# env block instead, then restart LM Studio.
# AGENT_TOKEN=tok_change_me_abc123

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging — all optional; these change what gets recorded. README §14.
# -------------------------------------------------------------

# Per-save log verbosity (memory_bridge.py / vector-skill.py):
#   0 = off (default)        1 = warnings
#   2 = warnings + errors    3 = + successful saves
#   4 = + full content copy (warns if content > 10 KB)
MEMORY_LOG_LEVEL=0

# Directory for per-tool logs (memory_bridge.log, vector_skill.log); the
# consolidation daemon merges them daily into shared_memory_YYYY-MM-DD.log.gz.
MEMORY_LOG_PATH=~/.shared-memory/logs

# REM outbox audit log (JSON-lines, one entry per reviewed outbox row),
# written by rem_loop.py. Empty = disabled (default). Never auto-rotated.
AUDIT_LOG_PATH=

# Gateway per-request audit log (JSON-lines: ts, agent, role, method, path,
# status, latency_ms, request_id; plus principal + connected_from when the client
# connects over the Unix socket), written by the auth middleware OFF the DB hot
# path. The observability tier of agent auditing; pair it with the DB for a full
# picture (README §14 — log = who/from-where, DB = which decision/project).
# Empty = disabled. Not rotated. become non-repudiable once PoP auth lands.
# GATEWAY_AUDIT_LOG_PATH=~/.shared-memory/logs/gateway-audit.jsonl

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — only set these if your deployment differs from the defaults
# -------------------------------------------------------------

# Person identity (the operator). The gateway reads the OS login account behind
# each connection from the kernel via SO_PEERCRED and stamps it as `principal`
# (+ a `connected_from` fingerprint) on every write and audit line — never from
# the request, so an agent cannot forge it. Requires connecting over the AF_UNIX
# listener below; over TCP the principal is honestly absent. README §14.
#
# Gateway Unix-socket path. Default $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/shared-memory-gw.sock (per-user,
# 0700 dir → only that user reaches it — right for a single-user box). For a
# multi-user gateway set a shared path and widen GATEWAY_UDS_MODE. Set ="" to disable.
# GATEWAY_UDS_PATH=
# GATEWAY_UDS_MODE=0600
#
# Reject write routes that arrive without a kernel-attested principal (i.e. force
# clients onto the Unix socket). Off by default so the TCP path keeps working during
# rollout; turn on once every writer is on the socket.
# GATEWAY_REQUIRE_PRINCIPAL=0
#
# Client side: the Unix socket memory_bridge connects over so its saves get a
# principal. Default auto-detects $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/shared-memory-gw.sock; set ="" to
# force TCP. Must match the gateway's GATEWAY_UDS_PATH.
# COORDINATOR_UDS=

# Network interface the gateway binds to. README §9 / SECURITY.md.
# Default 127.0.0.1 (localhost only). Set 0.0.0.0 ONLY behind an encrypted
# overlay (Tailscale / WireGuard / TLS) — bearer tokens are plaintext over HTTP.
# PROXY_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Where an agent reaches the gateway. README §10a (Remote Clients).
# Default http://localhost:8888 is correct for same-host and SSH-tunnel
# clients; override only if your tunnel maps a different local port.
# COORDINATOR_URL=http://localhost:8888

# Dreaming LLM sampling temperature (REM enrichment + NREM consolidation).
# Per-daemon: REM_TEMPERATURE / NREM_TEMPERATURE; DREAM_TEMPERATURE sets both. Default 0.6.
# Tune per local model: Gemma ~0.6, Mistral-3 Instruct/Qwen ~0.1, Mistral-3 Reasoning ~1.0.
# DREAM_TEMPERATURE=0.6

# Interval between NREM global density sweeps — the safety net that drains
# clusters which crossed the density threshold without a triggering save
# (e.g. NOTIFYs lost while the daemon was down). The first sweep runs on the
# first idle tick after startup. Default 3600.
# NREM_SWEEP_INTERVAL_SEC=3600

# ── Dream-cycle generation bounds and failure handling ───────────────────────
# Every reasoning call sets max_tokens, and a length-finish FAILS the unit: a
# truncated draft is never repair-salvaged, never persisted, never fed to a
# quality gate. Because a fixed bound plus a dead-letter cap would silently and
# permanently exclude any record or cluster that legitimately needs more room,
# the bound is widened ONCE and the call retried before the unit fails. Raise
# the base bound (not the factor) if your model is consistently more verbose.
# REM_MAX_TOKENS_SOLO=1500
# REM_MAX_TOKENS_PER_FACT=400
# REM_MAX_TOKENS_PER_SUMMARY=250
# REM_TRUNCATION_RETRY_FACTOR=2.0
# NREM's two fold prompts are CUMULATIVE — each is handed the previous summary /
# insight and told to expand it — so a fold must re-emit that whole narrative
# before adding one new record. The previous narrative's length is a hard FLOOR
# under these bounds, and it grows every time the fold succeeds. Set below that
# floor the fold cannot succeed at all: obeying the bound drops content and
# fails the preservation gate, obeying the gate overruns the bound and truncates
# — then the dead-letter cap drops the cluster. Busiest domains cross first.
# Raise these if a legitimately larger narrative truncates; truncation_failures
# on GET /memory/telemetry is the signal. The practical ceiling is generation
# TIME (LLM_CEILING_FLOOR), not the model's context window.
# NREM_MAX_TOKENS_SUMMARY=8192
# NREM_MAX_TOKENS_INSIGHT=8192
# NREM_TRUNCATION_RETRY_FACTOR=2.0

# Per-call hard ceiling (seconds) for every dream LLM call, so a valid long
# generation is never killed for being long. The effective ceiling is the
# LARGEST of: this floor, prompt_chars/100, work_units*15, and — since decode
# time is driven by the OUTPUT bound, not the input — max_tokens/LLM_MIN_TOK_S.
# LLM_CEILING_FLOOR=600

# Slowest generation rate (tokens/second) the ceiling will sit through. THIS IS
# HARDWARE, so it is the one value you should expect to change: set it from your
# own throughput, deliberately UNDER your observed floor rather than at your
# average — a ceiling sized on the average kills every slower-than-average run.
# Reference measurement on the development rig (Qwen3-14B Q4_K_M, one Intel Arc
# card, single slot): min 11.29, mean 13.93, max 15.10 tok/s -> shipped as 10.
# Raising a NREM_MAX_TOKENS_* bound without checking this is what turns "needs a
# longer narrative" into an opaque timeout that is never counted as a capacity
# failure. Keep max_tokens/LLM_MIN_TOK_S under NREM_FORCED_SLOT_WAIT (1800s) or
# a long fold outlasts REM's willingness to yield the shared LLM slot.
# LLM_MIN_TOK_S=10

# Poison-record escape hatch: a record whose enrichment fails this many times
# is dead-lettered — dropped from REM's queue until an operator resets
# n.rem_attempts. Only failures ATTRIBUTABLE TO THE RECORD count (its line was
# unparseable, its write failed); a transport failure such as a pool 503 says
# nothing about the record and is never charged. Watch rem_dead_lettered on
# GET /memory/telemetry — non-zero means enrichment is losing records.
# REM_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# REM naming authority. By default REM LINKS but never MINTS: when it proposes a
# relationship to a name that is not already a node, the name is dropped rather
# than created (the count appears in the journal as "REM mint gate"). This keeps
# entity creation on the capture path, where a human named the concept, instead
# of letting free-text extraction invent cluster keys — the observed failure mode
# was sentence fragments becoming Entity nodes and then polluting alias
# resolution and thematic-summary keys.
# Set to 1 if your capture surface does NOT name entities up front and you would
# rather REM discover them; that restores the older behaviour exactly.
# REM_MAY_MINT_ENTITIES=0

# REM entity grounding — two DIFFERENT sets, bounded separately.
#
# The names REM will ACCEPT from the LLM (the link gate above resolves against
# this) and the names it SHOWS the LLM in the prompt used to be one capped list.
# Past the cap, a known entity whose name sorted late fell out of BOTH: never
# offered, and rejected if named anyway — so the mention was dropped and that
# entity's cluster silently stopped growing toward the fold threshold.
#
# ENTITY_REGISTRY_LIMIT — the ACCEPT set: a safety valve, not a working limit.
#   The gate needs every typed node or it rejects names that exist. It is only a
#   dict of strings; keep it well above your node count. Hitting it logs a
#   warning and means "prune the graph", not "this is fine".
# ENTITY_PROMPT_K — the SHOW set: the k entities nearest to THIS record's text by
#   cosine over the entity-embedding store. Relevance-ranked instead of
#   alphabetical, so the prompt gets SMALLER as the graph grows, not larger.
#   Raise it if records routinely reference many entities at once.
# ENTITY_SET_LIMIT — the FALLBACK slice size, used when semantic recall is
#   unavailable (embedder unreachable, empty embedding store). Grounding matters
#   most during an outage, so recall degrades to the previous alphabetical
#   behaviour and never to an empty list.
# GROUNDING_EMBED_CAP — chars of a record's text sent to the embedder for ranking.
# ENTITY_REGISTRY_LIMIT=20000
# ENTITY_PROMPT_K=80
# ENTITY_SET_LIMIT=1500
# GROUNDING_EMBED_CAP=4000

# Fold dead-letter: a cluster that fails the preservation or truncation gate
# this many times within the window is skipped rather than re-folded forever.
# NREM_FOLD_FAIL_CAP=3
# NREM_FOLD_FAIL_WINDOW=7

# Preservation-gate corrective retries. The RULE itself (every decision/
# retrospective anchor is hard-required, zero coverage tolerance — the
# operator's core demand: no captured record may silently vanish from a
# synthesised narrative) is NOT controlled by this and does not change. This
# only bounds how many corrective attempts the gate gets before giving up —
# raised from 1 to 2 once measurement showed a decision cluster's anchor set
# is several independent tokens that must ALL match on the SAME attempt, so
# one retry's recovery probability compounds down fast as cluster size grows.
# NREM_PRESERVATION_MAX_RETRIES=2

# Preservation-gate slack floor. Soft (plain-fact) anchors tolerate a little
# paraphrase loss, budgeted as floor(cluster_size * 0.10) dropped anchors. That
# ratio quantises to ZERO for any cluster below ten records — and since the
# density threshold makes five-to-nine the ordinary cluster size, the tolerance
# never reached the common case, while a ten-record cluster got a free drop its
# nine-record neighbour did not. Clusters of at least this many records now get
# at least one droppable soft anchor. Decision/retrospective anchors remain
# hard-required regardless: this floor can never rescue one.
# NREM_PRESERVATION_SLACK_MIN_UNITS=5

# REM/NREM slot arbitration. The two dream daemons share one serial LLM slot.
# REM re-arms faster and its solo units run for minutes, so without an arbiter
# consolidation can defer indefinitely. NREM takes a well-known advisory lock
# while queuing for the slot and REM yields its turn. The wait budget MUST
# exceed your longest REM unit or the queue expires before REM ever finishes.
# Both daemons must agree on the lock key.
# NREM_FORCED_SLOT_WAIT=1800
# NREM_PRIORITY_ADVISORY_LOCK_KEY=8765310

# Idle window before a DUE event-driven consolidation fires (the hard backstop
# is 3x this). Default 900 = the documented 15-minute intent.
# NREM_IDLE_THRESHOLD_SEC=900

# How often NREM re-reads its eligibility predicate: the count of facts sitting
# at 'rem_reviewed' in the outbox, i.e. enriched by REM but not yet folded.
# Due-ness is a property of THAT ledger, never of the save notifications that
# happen to have arrived -- a save means a record was written, not that an
# eligible cluster exists. One cheap indexed read per interval; the interval
# also bounds how stale the observed backlog may be. Default 60.
# NREM_ELIGIBILITY_RECHECK_SEC=60

# How often NREM's idle clock probes the LLM pool. The clock that decides "the
# system is quiet" has to see the whole system: if it only counts saves, REM can
# hold the shared slot for twenty minutes while the clock runs on and NREM
# becomes due mid-batch. Fail-open like every other pool probe. Default 15.
# NREM_POOL_PROBE_SEC=15

# Project-name normalisation (decision 276). Canonical project = folder name.
# The coordinator rewrites metadata.project / decision.project through this
# alias map at ingress so the insight gate's >=2-distinct-projects rule is
# trustworthy. One-time backfill: scripts/normalize_projects.py --map "...".
# Example — adapt to your own folder names:
# PROJECT_ALIASES=shared_mem=my-project,shared_memory=my-project,old-name=my-project

# Insight consolidation mega-hub guard: entities whose total degree exceeds
# this cap never key a decision cluster (context only). Default 50.
# INSIGHT_HUB_DEGREE_CAP=50

# ADR-017 alias-writer (scripts/alias_writer.py) — Tier-2 (LLM-adjudicated)
# cadence (decision 852). Tier 1 (deterministic normalized-exact auto-accept)
# is always unconditional, never gated by this. Tier 2 runs whenever it has
# been at least this many hours since the last one — checked against the
# durable alias_adjudications ledger, not a separate marker, so `--auto` is
# safe to invoke on any schedule. Deliberately does NOT defer to REM/NREM
# pool business: alias-adjudication quality gates consolidation quality (a
# fragmented/wrongly-split entity graph degrades every downstream fold), so
# it is not treated as lower-priority than the dream cycles that depend on it.
# ALIAS_SWEEP_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# Concurrency for ADR-017 Tier-2 batch dispatch. With LLM_BACKENDS configuring
# more than one backend, dispatching batches concurrently is what lets the
# gateway's least-in-flight routing actually spread them across backends —
# sequential dispatch could never do that. Mirrors LLM_AFFINITY_MAX_INFLIGHT.
# ALIAS_LLM_MAX_CONCURRENT=4

# -------------------------------------------------------------
# Concurrency & load hardening — all optional, sane defaults shown.
# -------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres pool sizing. This is a SYSTEM budget: pg max_connections must cover
# POOL_MAX + the REM daemon (1) + NREM (per-op) + the LISTEN connection +
# headroom. POOL_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT bounds how long a request waits for a free
# connection before the gateway sheds it (503 + Retry-After) instead of hanging.
# POOL_MIN=2
# POOL_MAX=20
# POOL_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=5.0

# Neo4j driver pool (gateway + both daemons share Neo4j). Bounds queueing under
# concurrent search/daemon contention; acquisition timeout fails fast.
# NEO4J_MAX_POOL=50
# NEO4J_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=30.0

# Failed-outbox retry backoff (per row): next_attempt_at = now + base·2^retries
# (capped, jittered). Stops a Neo4j outage becoming a 2 s-cycle retry storm.
# OUTBOX_BACKOFF_BASE=2.0
# OUTBOX_BACKOFF_MAX=300.0

# Per-entity write-lock registry cap. Idle locks evict LRU past this bound so
# the map can't grow unbounded with unique entity names. Default 4096.
# LOCKS_MAX_SIZE=4096

# Outer load-shed valve: max concurrent in-flight requests at the auth seam
# (counts requests parked on slow embeddings/LLM too). 0 = disabled (default).
# GATEWAY_INFLIGHT_MAX=0

# Consolidation signal (ADR-018) — dream-cycle liveness on /health + telemetry.
# STALL_THRESHOLD defaults to 2.5× NREM_SWEEP_INTERVAL_SEC (the insight cycle
# rides every sweep) so a single deferred/failed sweep never trips the alert.
# ORPHAN_TIMEOUT: an in-flight run row older than this reads as a dead-mid-fold
# crash, not a live fold. RETENTION: the daemon prunes older run rows at startup.
# CONSOLIDATION_STALL_THRESHOLD_SEC=9000
# CONSOLIDATION_HEALTH_REFRESH_SEC=60
# CONSOLIDATION_ORPHAN_TIMEOUT_SEC=1800
# CONSOLIDATION_RUNS_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ── Backup (ops/backup.sh — gateway host only) ───────────────────────────────
# Policy (schedule/retention/destination) is YOURS; the script ships the mechanism.
# Mint an admin-role token (generate_tokens.py) and grant it the 'admin' role so
# backup.sh can quiesce the gateway over POST /admin/backup. An admin token is
# confined to /admin/* — it cannot read or write memory.
#   AGENT_TOKENS=...,backup:tok_backup_xxx
#   AGENT_ROLES=monitor:read,backup:admin
# BACKUP_ADMIN_TOKEN=tok_backup_xxx
# BACKUP_DIR=~/.shared-memory/backups
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# BACKUP_QUIESCE_MAX_SECONDS=900     # gateway TTL auto-resume if the script dies
# BACKUP_DRAIN_MAX_SECONDS=120       # wait for the outbox to drain before dumping
# BACKUP_QUIESCE_REQUIRED=0          # 1 = abort if the gateway can't be quiesced
# Advisory-lock key shared by gateway + REM + NREM (change all three together):
# BACKUP_ADVISORY_LOCK_KEY=8765309
# Container / DB names (compose defaults shown):
# PG_CONTAINER=postgres-vector
# NEO4J_CONTAINER=neo4j-memory

# Reasoning-LLM backend POOL (OPTIONAL). The gateway owns LLM routing + load
# parallelisation: clients only ever call the gateway (/v1/chat/completions) and
# never know how many models back it or where they run. List one OR MORE
# OpenAI-compatible backends — local or REMOTE host. Unset = a single backend at
# LLM_DEFAULT_TARGET (below). Selection is least-in-flight, so concurrent work
# (REM + NREM, or the future LLM-judge) fans out across free backends; a down
# backend is skipped for a short cooldown so one card always keeps serving.
# LLM_BACKENDS=http://localhost:5000,http://localhost:4000,http://remote-host:1234
#   each entry is a backend url (an optional url@weight is still honoured, but the
#   default equal weight is preferred). Dispatch is CACHE-AFFINITY first — a request
#   whose large prompt PREFIX is already warm on a backend (llama.cpp KV prefix-cache)
#   is routed back there (huge win for a big shared grounding prefix), then
#   least-in-flight while protecting warm cards from eviction. Allocation-free; no
#   precomputed capacity split. Judge/quality reservation is decided by the GATEWAY at
#   runtime (e.g. the periodical recheck reserves a card then releases it) — never env.
#
# LLM_BACKENDS_JSON — takes priority over LLM_BACKENDS above when set. Use this
# form instead when any backend needs its own credential (a paid cloud API) or
# its own model id; the plain comma form has no way to carry either.
# LLM_BACKENDS_JSON=[{"url":"http://localhost:5000"},
#                     {"url":"https://api.deepseek.com/v1",
#                      "token_env":"DEEPSEEK_API_KEY","model":"deepseek-chat"}]
#   "token_env" NAMES an env var the gateway process must already have —
#   NEVER a literal key here or anywhere else this framework writes to disk.
#   Resolved once at startup and sent as Authorization ONLY to that backend;
#   a client's own gateway auth token is never forwarded to any backend. A
#   named var that isn't actually set in the environment excludes that backend
#   from the pool (logged) rather than sending a doomed request. "model"
#   overrides LLM_MODEL below for that backend only. Full credential-handling
#   guidance (keep the raw key in an encrypted store, never in a file) is in
#   shared-memory/ops/README.md, "Reasoning-LLM backends" section.
# LLM_FAIL_THRESHOLD=2   # backend fails within LLM_FAIL_WINDOW → cooldown
# LLM_FAIL_WINDOW=60
# LLM_COOLDOWN=300       # seconds a failing backend is ejected before re-probe
# LLM_AFFINITY_PREFIX_CHARS=6144   # prompt-prefix length hashed for the affinity key
# LLM_AFFINITY_TTL=600             # seconds a warm-prefix→backend mapping is trusted
# LLM_AFFINITY_MAX_INFLIGHT=4      # prefer the warm card unless its queue exceeds this

# Single-backend fallback, used ONLY when LLM_BACKENDS is unset. Ports differ by
# stack — LM Studio defaults to :1234, llama.cpp servers commonly :8080 — so set
# this to whatever YOUR reasoning endpoint is rather than inheriting a default.
# LLM_DEFAULT_TARGET=http://localhost:5000

# Model id sent on every reasoning call. "local-model" suits llama.cpp / LM Studio,
# which ignore the field. Set the REAL id for any backend that validates it: vLLM or
# TGI with named models, an OpenRouter/LiteLLM router, an OpenAI-compatible cloud
# endpoint, or LM Studio with several models loaded.
# LLM_MODEL=local-model

# Embedding + reranking endpoints the GATEWAY forwards to. These move the ENDPOINT
# (other ports, another container network, a remote host) — clients never call them
# directly, they always go through the gateway.
#
# NOTE — the embedding/reranking MODELS are part of the fixed contract for now, not a
# flexible surface. The 1024-dim BGE-M3 vector is baked into the schema (vector(1024)
# in 000_base_schema.sql and 014_alias_resolution.sql), there is no dimension guard,
# and once data exists a different-dimension model requires a full RE-EMBED, not a
# config change. Only the reasoning LLM above is freely swappable today.
# EMBEDDER_URL=http://localhost:8070
# RERANKER_URL=http://localhost:8071

# Reference resolution (Stage 1.2b) — materialises textual record cross-references
# ("refines decision 381") as INFORMED_BY / REFERENCES edges. The link is always
# deterministic; only the relationship-TYPE judgment is configurable. The judge is
# the weaker model, so it is gated to the ambiguous Decision->Decision case only and
# its output is strictly validated, with a deterministic fallback.
# REFERENCE_JUDGE_MODE=deterministic       # deterministic (default) | llm
# REFERENCE_JUDGE_URL=http://localhost:5000/v1   # OpenAI-compatible base; e.g. http://atlas:1234/v1 to offload to a separate node
# REFERENCE_JUDGE_MODEL=local-model
